Key names: an open problem
==========================

The problem
===========

A `KeyStroke` wraps a 'key name', a Str.
`KeyStroke#` accepts a Str only when `_KeyNames.isName` holds.
`rt/gui/_KeyNames$b4$0.java` builds the set of 'key names' from the names of the `VK_` fields of `java.awt.event.KeyEvent`, without `VK_`, by reflection on the running JDK.
`rt/gui/CKeyManager.java` runs the action of a `KeyStroke` when the AWT key code of a key press is the value of the `VK_` field of that 'key name'.

So today:
- The set of 'key names' is the set of the running JDK: a JDK adding or removing a `VK_` field changes which Fearless programs run.
- The error of `KeyStroke#` names `java.awt.event.KeyEvent`, a Java class: `KeyStroke#"NOPE" is not a key name. Key names are the names of the VK_ constants of java.awt.event.KeyEvent without VK_, for example "A", "7", "SPACE", "ENTER", "ESCAPE", "BACK_SPACE", "LEFT", "F1", "SHIFT".`
- No Fearless documentation lists the 'key names'.
- The AWT key code of a key press depends on the OS and on the keyboard layout.
- A `KeyStroke` is a single key: there is no name for a key combination, like a modifier with a letter.

The goal
========

The design forges an OS and keyboard independent API, where each reasonable key combination has a specific and intuitive string name.
The list of names is owned by Fearless: the standard library defines the list, and the running JDK does not change the list.
The error for a Str that is not a name lists the names, or lists the rules that build the names, in full.

Points the design covers
========================

Modifiers.
`Frame.onKey` gives a handler only `.keyStroke`: a handler cannot tell Ctrl+S from S.
While a TextField or a TextArea has the focus, Ctrl with a letter other than the six text shortcuts (A, C, X, V, Z, Y) reaches `Frame.onKey` as the plain letter: a binding on S runs when the user presses Ctrl+S in a TextField.

Repeated presses.
`CKeyManager` drops the repeated presses of a held key: a handler cannot follow the auto repeat of the desk, for example to keep moving while an arrow key is held.

The name "UNDEFINED".
`KeyStroke#"UNDEFINED"` is a key name (`VK_UNDEFINED`, key code 0), and it is the default value of `KeyStroke`.
A binding on it runs for every key the toolkit cannot name, and `CapGui.closeKey(KeyStroke#"UNDEFINED")` closes the window on such keys.
`CKeyManager` pairs held keys by key code and location, so two different keys with code 0 count as one held key: the press of the second is dropped while the first is held.

macOS (https://github.com/FearlessLang/StandardLibrary/issues/366).
On macOS the third level of a keyboard layout comes from Option, which Java reports as Alt with no AltGraph.
`CTextKeys.typesWith` drops the characters typed with Option: `@ [ ] { } | \ ~` on German, French and Italian layouts, `© π €` on the U.S. layout.
The text shortcuts use Control and never Command; on macOS they are Command+A, C, X, V, Z and Shift+Command+Z.
Not checked on a Mac: there is no Mac desk.
